5. Kxa3 { +he would lose by the Black d-Pawn advancing } 5... d3
{ +backed up by the King. } ) 5... Kf5 6. Kxa3 Kxg5 7. Kb4 Kf6 8. a4
{ +[This ending involves nice calculation of distances on both sides. The rule for stopping a passed Pawn on an open board is that the adverse King must stand "within the quadrangle of four equal sides formed by the square the passed Pawn stands upon and the queening square." (Freeborough.) When, as in this case, the Pawn can move two squares on its first move, the King must be able to move first even when just within the quadrangle. Here the quadrangle for the a-Pawn is the box formed by a8-a4-e4-e8. Because the Black King can step into that box on the move, the Pawn can be caught by the Black King.] }
8... d3 $10
{ +[Drawn. Here the quadrangle for the d-Pawn is the box formed by d1-d3-b3-b1. Because the White King can step into that box on the move, the Pawn can be caught by the White King.] }
